 { There are a few tactical themes that could have been included in this book without seeming out of place. The desperado is an obvious one to name. But desperado scenarios are relatively rare and do not fit in a small list of calculation techniques. However, almost all complicated tactical games include some kind of intermediate moves, if not in the actual play, then at least in the heads of the players. The idea is simple and not unlike comparison. By inserting a move at a relevant time you change the properties of the position a little bit and they are suddenly in your favour. A closely related technique, which I would rank as being in the outskirts of calculation mainstream techniques, is trying to change the move order. Sometimes they are so closely related that you cannot tell them apart. The first example is a bit like that. Tamaz Gelashvili – Alexander Goldin, Orlando 2011 Black has just taken a pawn on d5 with the idea that } ( 20. Nxd5 $2 { would be met with } Rxe2 { and 21...Rxb2. White needs to look for something else, and all he could come up with was the rather unenergetic } ) 
 ( 20. Bxh7 $6 Kxh7 21. Rxd5 { After this both players made additional mistakes before Black won on move 39. At this point, the best move was } Kg8 $1 { with even chances. } ) 
 { But let us try to imagine that White had spent a minute or so looking for other options, and realized that he had an opportunity to start an attack on the kingside. } 20. Rc4 $1 { This energetic move would have forced Black to enter complications, as all normal-looking moves are easily refuted. Retreating with the queen along the b8-h2 diagonal ends in disaster on the kingside after both Bxh7+ and the less rushed, but not really less energetic Ng5. In sort of the same way, } ( 20. Rc4 Qh6 21. Rh4 { leads to immediate disaster, so with no retreats available, Black needs to push forward. } ) 
Rxe3 { If we continue to think that White was looking for candidate moves and saw his last move, probably he also saw that this reply was forced. But at this point, he might have decided to throw out the line, as } ( 20... Rxe3 20. fxe3 Qxe3 $15 { is pretty bleak. After the exchange of queens the knight will arrive on e3 and Black wins the exchange back. } ) 
 { But White has a better option available. } 21. Bxh7+ $3 { You can discuss whether it is 20.Rc4 or this that is the intermediate move. Chess is played move by move, but our way of thinking is in concepts. Here the idea is either to get the rook up to the fourth rank before executing the planned capture on h7 and recapture on d5 – or it is a planned capture on h7 with an intermediate rook swing to the fourth, forcing Black to give up an exchange? It is all up to how you think and, in this case, neither way can be considered better than the other. } Kh8 { If Black now takes the bishop with } ( 21... Kxh7 { then White has } 22. Qc2 { almost winning the queen. Black can resist with } Re4 { but White is better: } 23. Rxe4 Nce7 24. Qd3 Qf5 25. Nd4 Nf4 26. Rxf4 Qxd3 27. Rxd3 $16 ) 
22. fxe3 Qxe3+ 23. Qf2 $1 { This move can also easily be overlooked, but it is actually not so mysterious. Black is not allowed to put a knight on e3. } Bc5 { A natural move and also the best defensive try. Whether Black takes on f2 first or plays } ( 23... Kxh7 24. Rxd5 Qxf2 { does not really matter. The line is the same: } 25. Kxf2 Bc5 26. Rcxc5 $1 { The easiest is to return the exchange for clarity, but it is not forced. } ( 25... Bc5 25. Ke1 $14 { is also quite okay, if somewhat inferior. } ) 
bxc5 27. Rd7 Nd8 28. Ng5 Kg6 29. Nxf7 $1 Nxf7 30. Rxb7 $16 { White has good winning chances with his strongly placed rook and better minor piece. Black’s drawing chances are made up by the passed pawn and the relatively small number of remaining pawns. } ) 
24. Rxc5 { This time around White does not have a choice. } Qxc5 ( 24... bxc5 25. Rxd5 { transposes to 23...Kxh7 as discussed above. } ) 
25. Bf5 $1 { Here there is a small yet important difference from the 23...Kxh7 line. After } ( 25. Qxc5 $6 bxc5 26. Rxd5 Kxh7 27. Rd7 Nd8 28. Ng5 Kg6 29. Nxf7 Nxf7 30. Rxb7 { it would appear that we have the same position until you notice that the king is on g1 instead of f2. Actually, this difference is quite important. Black is in time to play } Rd8 $1 { and generate counterplay against the white king and white bishop. Black should draw without too much hassle. } ) 
Qxf2+ 26. Kxf2 Rd8 27. Ng5 Kg8 28. Ne6 $1 fxe6 { The lesser evil. After } ( 28... Rd6 29. Nxg7 $16 { Black faces all kinds of tactical problems, not least of all the pin down the d-file. } ) 
29. Bxe6+ Kf8 30. Rxd5 Rxd5 31. Bxd5 Ne5 32. Bxb7 Nd3+ 33. Ke3 Nxb2 34. Kd4 { White has definite winning chances in this ending. His king is very active and he has the possibility of creating a passed pawn on the kingside. However, I still believe that the ending is within drawing limits, not least because Black can hope for an ending with a bishop and h-pawn, which White will not be able to win. So, even though this elegant tactical idea does not lead to a forced win, it would have provided Black with great problems just to get to this unpleasant ending. In practical terms, this would mean time trouble, poor defence and probably a white victory. } 
 *

