 { The last example shows quite a normal scenario for intermediate moves and why it is so important to have them included as part of your training regimen. Kjetil Lie – Sune Berg Hansen, Porto Carras 2011 In the game, White played } ( 18. Ne3 $11 { and was outplayed gradually until he lost on move 49. } ) 
18. Nxh6+ $1 { Obviously, this is one of the first moves a grandmaster would look at, but without the right follow-up the attraction is rather limited. For example: } ( 18. Nxh6+ gxh6 19. Qxf6 hxg5 20. Bxg5 { offers White some compensation, but Black is generally all right. And this is the case with all lines that include looking exclusively on the kingside. } ) 
gxh6 19. b3 $3 { A very difficult move that neither of the players considered during the game. Their focus was simply elsewhere, which shows the potential power of having intermediate moves in your arsenal. Notice that 18 b3 did not work either. Black can retreat the bishop to e6, or first take on f5 and when White recaptures with the pawn play ...Bd5. Again there could be a discussion about whether b2-b3 or the knight sacrifice is the intermediate move. And again it would be a bit like discussing whether it is the drums or the bass which is out of sync. The thinking is that of intermediate moves, caring about the labelling is the thinking of... well, let’s just not go there. At this point, Black has various options, all of them leading to a clear advantage for White. We shall look at them individually. } ( 19. b3 Be6 20. Qxf6 { gives White a great position. Now } Ng6 $16 { is objectively best, but obviously not what Black would generally consider desirable. The fantastic point behind the whole operation is that } ( 20. b3 hxg5 $2 { is met with: } 21. Qh6 $3 { White’s attack on the dark squares is tremendous and Black cannot come up with an adequate defence. For example: } g4 22. Bg5 f6 23. Bxf6 Rxf6 24. Qxf6 Bd7 25. O-O-O { and White’s attack is irresistible. } ) 
 ) 
 ( 19. b3 Qd6 { is rather defeatist, but is probably the best move. White is more than comfortable after } 20. bxc4 bxc4 21. Nh3 Kg7 22. Rb1 $16 { but the game continues. } ) 
Kg7 20. bxc4 hxg5 $6 { This also cannot be recommended. The last chance would be } ( 20... Qd6 21. Nh3 bxc4 22. Rb1 { which transposes to 19...Qd6. White is close to winning. } ) 
21. hxg5 Nh7 { Black is also in big trouble after } ( 21... Nd7 22. cxb5 axb5 23. Bb3 Ng6 24. Rh7 $1 { and White has a winning attack. } ) 
22. Bb3 Ng6 ( 22... Qd6 23. Rh6 Ng6 { is another defensive try, but simple play with } 24. cxb5 axb5 25. Bd2 { and 26.0–0–0 gives a winning attack. } ) 
23. Rxh7+ Kxh7 24. Qh5+ Kg8 25. c5 $1 { White wins. Notice the double effect of the last move, preventing ...Qd6 at the same time as the bishop’s diagonal is opened. } 
